+Here the system parameter $t$ is time in seconds and dot denotes $d/dt$; $m$ is a positive mass parameter (kilograms in the SI interpretation), $c$ a positive speed parameter (metres per second), and $\kappa$ a positive energy-times-length parameter (joule-metres). Define the radial momentum $p_r:=x\cdot p/r$ and angular-momentum vector $J:=x\times p$. A trajectory is a $C^1$ solution of this ODE on an interval; a solution is maximal when it has no extension as a $C^1$ solution in the stated phase domain to a strictly larger connected interval containing its initial time. The IVP and interval-solution convention is [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]].
 
 This is only a mathematical phase system. Its physical interpretation as a charged particle in an externally prescribed Coulomb field requires separate model assumptions.
